    "stem": "Madhav works on a multiplication cryptarithm. Each letter stands for one digit, different letters stand for different digits, and the first digit of a multi-digit number is never $0$. He wants to narrow possible digits before trying every full substitution.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c} & & A \\\\ \\times & & B \\\\ \\hline & C & D \\end{array}$$\n\nWhich claim correctly narrows a digit?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "$A$ and $B$ cannot be $0$, and the product $CD$ is a two-digit number, so $A\\times B$ is between $10$ and $99$.",
      "B": "Letter $C$ must equal letter $A$, with no further constraint from the product size.",
      "C": "Any digits for $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ work as long as each letter is a single digit, including $0$ for a leading place.",
      "D": "The product $CD$ must be smaller than one factor $A$."
    },
